The core of it:

```python
# one bassline at a time: A to the boundary, B from it, 50 ms equal-power blend centred on h
bass, ga, gb = dsp.bass_handover(bass_A, bass_B, L // 2, int(SR * 0.05))
# kick (<150 Hz) linear: it is coherent on the shared grid. percussion equal-power: it is not.
drums = dsp.drums_crossfade(drums_A, drums_B, SR)
# generation: stop on non-finite, label the path, never let a fallback look like a result
gen = gen.numpy(); dsp.assert_finite(gen, f"transition {i} other")
other, rec["other_gain"] = dsp.match_rms(gen, other_ref)       # other_ref = plain crossfade of the real `other` windows
```

```python
# in dual_latch_guided_generate, per step
_check_finite(v_a, "v_a", i); _check_finite(v_b, "v_b", i)
step_loss = chroma_loss_rung1(features.transpose(1, 2), chroma_target_tensor.transpose(1, 2),
                              latent_fps, w_sec=w_sec, metric="cosine")
grad = torch.autograd.grad(step_loss, x_in)[0]; _check_finite(grad, "latch grad", i)
```

What the toy test establishes, and no more: with `v = x - d` as the model, the final head loss is lowest with
`+gain`, middle at gain 0, highest at `-gain`, so the sign of the guidance in your loop is right. The toy also showed a
real hazard: a cosine loss on a near-zero `x0_pred` gives a gradient of order 1e5, which the new guard catches.

