My earlier entries (Phase 0 review, sync checks, `base_a2a`) are merged with your new code, not replaced by it. All numbers below are
from the laptop's Arc (fp32, `medium-base`, no adapter), c01 -> c02, 8-bar window.

**1. An offset bug that the real model showed, and the likely cause of "the clips sounded offset before mixing" with an independently
written sine schedule.** The SAME autoencoder floors the latent count and CROPS THE FRONT of an input that is not a whole number of
4096-sample frames. Measured: N = 151 x 4096 + 3314 (the 14.1 s window) encodes to 151 frames and decodes 618 496 samples, early by
exactly the 3314 dropped samples (75.1 ms), identically on two different clips; N = 151 x 4096 gives zero shift. `generate` pads the END
itself, so a flat a2a measures lag 0 at both lengths. The sine schedule encodes the un-padded window for its reference latents
(`pre.encode(other_ref)`, in `cb2` and in my version alike), so with N mod 4096 = 3314 the held frames carry content 75 ms out of
time: measured on the real model, a sine-schedule a2a at that length came back **-3314 samples (-75.1 ms, correlation 0.59)**,
the flat one 0 and the sine one at a whole-frame length 0. `latent_slerp_other` has the same defect: **-92.8 ms** against the real
onsets in its first real run (your `d-xfade_o-slerp`). A window of 8 bars at 136 BPM is 621 810 samples, so almost every window hits it.
Fix: `dsp.pad_end_to_frame` before every direct `pre.encode` (reference latents, slerp), decode trimmed. Re-measured on the real model:
sine schedule lag 0 at both lengths (correlation 0.89, the same as flat), slerp 0.0 ms. The fake codec in the tests now floors and
front-crops like the real one, and removing the padding fails the tests. Anything else that calls `pre.encode` on unpadded audio has it.

**2. The sync verdict was fooled by one bar.** In the 8-bar window the A-vs-B kick offsets were [2.8, 1.2, -4.8, 0.0, -3.2, -2.0,
**+102.4**, -4.2] ms: seven bars within 5 ms and one where the kicks do not pair up (a fill or break). A least-squares drift
read +3.63 ms/s, the transition was called unsynced and the kick ramp flipped to equal-power. `dsp.drift_ms_per_s` is now the robust
Theil-Sen slope and `dsp.grid_verdict` needs at least 80 % of the bars within tolerance (one in eight or in five is an outlier note,
`ab_outlier_bars`; with 2-4 bars every bar must pass). Re-run: synced, drift -0.23 ms/s, kick ramp linear, bar 7 reported.

**3. The drum inpaint (`799b349`) is not reliably on the grid with the base model, and its output was never checked.**
`inpaint_grid_check` fits the grid to the kicks outside the mask and measures the kicks inside. Two runs, same inputs and seed
(XPU runs are not deterministic; do not trust one): 2-bar, run 1: 8/8 kicks, max 36 ms, median 25 ms; run 2: 7/8 kicks, max 209 ms,
median 38 ms. 4-bar, run 1: 16/16, max 122 ms; run 2: 14/16, max 216 ms, median 8.6 ms. The REAL kicks in the same spans deviate by
max 35-42 ms, median 30-33 (swing; your vintage-goa finding), so a flat 30 ms would have failed the real material; the limit is
max(30 ms, 1.5 x the real maximum) and a span with fewer than 75 % of the expected kicks also fails. All four are flagged; with
`--require-sync` the run stops. The plan's "kick intervals across the bridge" check is automated.

**4. The rest of the review.**
